WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.664
MLX on Apple Silicon.

2
00:00:01.944 --> 00:00:07.192
MLX is Apple’s open source array framework for machine learning on Apple silicon.

3
00:00:07.472 --> 00:00:18.587
Its README highlights the design choice that matters here: arrays live in unified memory, so CPU and GPU operate on the same data without copying it between them.

4
00:00:18.867 --> 00:00:22.792
mlx-lm builds LLM inference and fine-tuning on top.

5
00:00:23.072 --> 00:00:26.315
pip install mlx-lm gives you mlx_lm.

6
00:00:26.595 --> 00:00:28.451
generate and mlx_lm.

7
00:00:28.731 --> 00:00:46.437
chat on the command line, and a Python API in which load returns a model and tokenizer, and stream_generate yields responses that report prompt_tps (prefill tokens per second), generation_tps (decode tokens per second), and peak_memory in GB.

8
00:00:46.717 --> 00:00:47.848
mlx_lm.

9
00:00:48.128 --> 00:01:00.267
convert with -q quantizes a Hugging Face model, by default to 4 bits with a group size of 64, and the mlx-community organization on the Hub hosts many models already converted.

10
00:01:00.547 --> 00:01:01.677
mlx_lm.

11
00:01:01.957 --> 00:01:11.131
server offers an HTTP API modeled on OpenAI’s chat API, which its docs say is not recommended for production.

12
00:01:11.411 --> 00:01:23.997
On a Mac, MLX and the GGUF runtimes are both reasonable; which is faster for a given model and quantization is an empirical question that changes with releases, so measure both on your machine.

13
00:01:24.277 --> 00:01:34.560
The WWDC25 session below, from Apple’s MLX team, walks through running, quantizing, and fine-tuning LLMs with mlx-lm.
